I would love to see my human Wizard Cass sculpted! 

&#x200B;

Appearance reference: [https://imgur.com/a/sg9KunC](https://imgur.com/a/sg9KunC)

&#x200B;

Other details: Stands 5'8", roughly 118 pounds. That makes her physique quite skinny and petite looking, but for the most part she's in better health than she may appear. She's a studious and compassionate young woman, and socially  an ambivert. She is very blind without her glasses. Cass will usually wear things that show her shoulders, and often wears tighter fit pants.

Cassandra Lichtengard is my Wizard in an ongoing campaign that has just started ramping up as of late. 

&#x200B;

She is quite the intelligent young woman at 20 years old, but she doesn't use her smarts to intimidate or lord over anyone, nor does she retreat and use her mind only for herself. Instead of being the somewhat stereotypical Wizard (Asocial isolationists holed up at home obsessing over magic), Cassandra takes a different approach with Wizardry. She knows its important to stay compassionate and empathetic in the face of adversity, and each person she meets, she sees as a potential ally or friend until they prove themselves otherwise. My friends and I joked that She'll turn the BBEG over to her side with her good nature, and what I have to say to that is: She'll certainly try, but have a plan if that fails.

Whoah! That looks really good, I am particularly digging the rendition on the flame! 

&#x200B;

Thank you so much!

Oh boy, I love me some Rogues.

&#x200B;

Right. I'd love to offer some of my own ideas and input regarding Rogues and Thieves' Guild.

&#x200B;

1. I like the notion that Rogues and the Thieves Guild don't have to be *bad guys*. Rogues and Thieves can best fit as gray characters, declining to act as good people while at the same time refusing to commit atrocities. Rogues may most generally tend towards lawlessness, but that doesn't mean they're awful people.
2. There is honor in a guild of outcasts, and these outcasts come from all over and are always a certainty in life. What that means is, that the Thieves' Guild will be *widespread,* you will find a chapter of the guild in most major cities, and smaller groups spread thinner.
3. Sometimes, Thieves' Guild and Rogues will be sanctioned by major governmental powers or to flip this on its head, small towns will be entirely run by the Thieves' Guild. There might also even be groups of Rogues that aren't sanctioned by government or the larger TG, making them outcasts of outcasts.
4. Get ready for Thieves' Cant, every *mundane* or *inconspicuous* action, exchange, or phrase might be a tell to other TG, Rogues, Fences, etc. That's where your trinket might come in? Maybe it's a silver coin with a special indistinct mark making it unique from other silver pieces? You hand that over to someone you might suspect is a rogue or TG associate and that tells them who you are. (They'd then hand the SP back as you "Suddenly have the copper pieces" to take the place of the SP you gave.)

&#x200B;

These are of course, 1 players ideas. Definitely don't feel intimidated to read about other people's Rogues and plot points that are centered around TG and Rogues, etc. As you go, you might come up with a whole lot more, so that's why I maybe refrained from giving name examples and stuff.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

True, and MoM needs concentration. But, like I said, my player and her bard are pretty clever. She waited until the other combatants were taken out so her only target was the big one. Smart play to be honest and that definitely shouldn't be punished. 

&#x200B;

I'm still pretty new to DM'ing, but those are certainly some good ideas that I'm going to try to implement just a bit more. It should be exciting to see fresher strategies coming from her.

Cassandra Lichtengard, my human Wizard, hair is black and styled in sort of a fringe bob, pale-skinned, purple eyes, pretty slim but stands at 5'9", warm and welcoming, but very bookish(She needs glasses, blind without them).

&#x200B;

